    {
      "id": 21,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "How to read this dashboard",
      "datasource": null,
      "gridPos": { "h": 8, "w": 24, "x": 0, "y": 60 },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "## Negative-effect signals\n\n| Signal | Meaning | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| `refault/s` and `activate/s` sustained > 0 | Reused file pages are being evicted and re-read: the cache is smaller than the working set | raise that instance's `limits.memory` |\n| `steal/s` sustained | Reclaim is repeatedly discarding cache | raise limit or accept lower cache hit rate |\n| `major faults/s` rising | Disk reads on the fault path | raise cache limit or move data |\n| `memory max events/s` > 0 | Cgroup is pinned at its hard limit | check whether `current` is mostly file cache (reclaimable) or `anon` (real need) |\n| `swap max events/s` / `swap fail/s` > 0 | Swap cap too small | raise `limits.memory.swap` or the hard limit |\n| `OOM kills` > 0 | Hard limit too small for peak anon + swap | raise limit/swap |\n| PSI `memory full` / `io full` sustained > 0 | Latency pressure | raise limits, reduce workloads, or add RAM |\n| NVMe write rate tracking swap activity | Disk swap or zram writeback is wearing the SSD | move back to zram-only or reduce pressure |\n\n`active_file` is the best single-number estimate of the file working set an instance reuses; `inactive_file` with zero refaults is cold and can be reclaimed. NVMe wear is now exposed from SMART (`percentage_used`, `available_spare`, lifetime data units written); current zram swap is RAM-only, so swap should not increase the SMART write counter. SMART counters update hourly, while kernel diskstats shows short bursts."
      }
    }
  ]
